WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.415 --> 00:00:01.984
- There are dumb questions.

00:00:01.984 --> 00:00:04.981
There are questions that
waste your time and my time

00:00:04.981 --> 00:00:06.808
and everybody else's time.

00:00:06.808 --> 00:00:10.975
And I've never had a job in my
life that I was better than.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.041
I was always just lucky to have a job.

00:00:14.041 --> 00:00:16.526
Being able to create

00:00:16.526 --> 00:00:20.544
an impulse within a consumer
to do something is an art form.

00:00:20.544 --> 00:00:23.152
If you want to be Mark Zuckerberg,

00:00:23.152 --> 00:00:26.514
the best you're ever going
to be is second place,

00:00:26.514 --> 00:00:28.496
'cause Mark Zuckerberg will always be

00:00:28.496 --> 00:00:31.029
a better Mark Zuckerberg than you.

00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:33.464
- He's an American actor and investor.

00:00:33.464 --> 00:00:34.794
He began his career as a model

00:00:34.794 --> 00:00:36.722
and started acting as Michael Kelso

00:00:36.722 --> 00:00:39.077
on the Fox sitcom, That '70s Show.

00:00:39.077 --> 00:00:40.572
In 2004, he starred

00:00:40.572 --> 00:00:43.312
in the psychological
film The Butterfly Effect

00:00:43.312 --> 00:00:45.054
and gained even more attention.

00:00:45.054 --> 00:00:46.956
He's Ashton Kutcher, and here's my take

00:00:46.956 --> 00:00:48.817
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:50.560
Rule number 10 is my personal favorite,

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.289
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:52.289 --> 00:00:53.793
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:53.793 --> 00:00:54.744
And as always, if he says

00:00:54.744 --> 00:00:56.509
something that really resonates with you,

00:00:56.509 --> 00:00:58.771
make sure to put it in the
comments and put quotes around it

00:00:58.771 --> 00:01:01.189
so other people can be inspired, as well.

00:01:01.189 --> 00:01:03.930
(loud whoosh)

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.847
(orchestral music)

00:01:14.303 --> 00:01:15.798
- There are dumb questions.

00:01:15.798 --> 00:01:18.931
There are questions that
waste your time and my time

00:01:18.931 --> 00:01:20.996
and everybody else's time.

00:01:20.996 --> 00:01:24.508
I started thinking, "Well,
what is a great question?

00:01:24.508 --> 00:01:28.091
"What constitutes an
exceptional question?"

00:01:31.553 --> 00:01:33.742
And I was thinking about it

00:01:33.742 --> 00:01:35.441
and grinding on it and thinking about it,

00:01:35.441 --> 00:01:37.714
and what I realized is
that a great question

00:01:37.714 --> 00:01:40.704
is a question that's armed with knowledge,

00:01:40.704 --> 00:01:43.013
that's armed with care and thought,

00:01:43.013 --> 00:01:45.706
and that pierces into the gray matter

00:01:45.706 --> 00:01:48.753
of the world and the
universe that we live in

00:01:48.753 --> 00:01:52.920
and looks for problems and
inconsistency and broken things.

00:01:56.417 --> 00:01:58.239
Great questions cause us

00:01:58.239 --> 00:02:02.200
to sit for days and ponder a solution.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.522
Great questions expose problems,

00:02:05.522 --> 00:02:09.515
and problems are the seeds to solutions.

00:02:09.515 --> 00:02:12.432
Great questions expose road blocks,

00:02:13.449 --> 00:02:16.782
which are an entrepreneur's opportunity.

00:02:18.222 --> 00:02:22.139
A great question sometimes
will arrive at a no,

00:02:24.491 --> 00:02:28.074
which is the playground
for a rebel spirit.

00:02:29.017 --> 00:02:31.802
And I have the great
opportunity in my life

00:02:31.802 --> 00:02:33.958
to work with a lot of
unbelievable entrepreneurs

00:02:33.958 --> 00:02:36.487
that are solving extraordinary problems,

00:02:36.487 --> 00:02:39.457
and the one thing that
they all have in common

00:02:39.457 --> 00:02:42.790
is they all ask extraordinary questions.

00:02:43.784 --> 00:02:47.192
And they build their businesses
from these questions.

00:02:47.192 --> 00:02:51.407
They build technology
from great questions,

00:02:51.407 --> 00:02:54.926
questions that parse
apart existing businesses.

00:02:54.926 --> 00:02:56.242
They tear them down.

00:02:56.242 --> 00:03:00.665
They tear down conventions
that we just assume are true.

00:03:00.665 --> 00:03:04.961
And they expose this
magical thing that exists

00:03:04.961 --> 00:03:07.794
behind every great business, time,

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:14.217
that most magical resource in the world.

00:03:14.217 --> 00:03:17.238
They find ways to save time.

00:03:17.238 --> 00:03:19.684
I think time exists on two vectors.

00:03:19.684 --> 00:03:22.504
It exists on one vector
which is longevity.

00:03:22.504 --> 00:03:24.708
It's a vector that goes on and on and on,

00:03:24.708 --> 00:03:26.246
and we're always in our lives

00:03:26.246 --> 00:03:29.834
seeking longer lives, better lives.

00:03:29.834 --> 00:03:32.746
It exists on another
vector, which is quality.

00:03:32.746 --> 00:03:35.701
We want better lives,
lives that are more filled

00:03:35.701 --> 00:03:39.368
with wonder and enjoyment
and entertainment.

00:03:40.993 --> 00:03:43.197
Those are the two vectors of time,

00:03:43.197 --> 00:03:46.376
and those are the two vectors
that every single business,

00:03:46.376 --> 00:03:48.991
every single great business, exposes,

00:03:48.991 --> 00:03:52.574
and they expose it
through great questions.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:57.026
I think that if I could give
young Hollywood a suggestion,

00:03:57.026 --> 00:03:58.457
it's to do something that I never did,

00:03:58.457 --> 00:04:01.208
which is have the courage to stand up

00:04:01.208 --> 00:04:02.751
and say what you believe

00:04:02.751 --> 00:04:06.084
and be responsible for your actions now.

00:04:06.986 --> 00:04:10.013
Learning from the bad things (mumbles)

00:04:10.013 --> 00:04:12.988
A big part of growing up in this business,

00:04:12.988 --> 00:04:14.745
which in a lot of ways I did,

00:04:14.745 --> 00:04:17.685
was you spend a lot of time
just trying to be liked,

00:04:17.685 --> 00:04:20.212
and you get really worried about,

00:04:20.212 --> 00:04:21.418
"Do people like what I did?"

00:04:21.418 --> 00:04:24.002
or, "Do people appreciate my work?"

00:04:24.002 --> 00:04:27.249
because that is your paycheck.

00:04:27.249 --> 00:04:29.198
And I think that having the guts

00:04:29.198 --> 00:04:32.726
to just be yourself sometimes
is harder than people think.

00:04:32.726 --> 00:04:36.893
I believe that opportunity
looks a lot like hard work.

00:04:38.213 --> 00:04:40.745
When I was 13, I had my
first job with my dad,

00:04:40.745 --> 00:04:42.992
carrying shingles up to the roof,

00:04:42.992 --> 00:04:46.912
and then I got a job washing
dishes at a restaurant,

00:04:46.912 --> 00:04:50.006
and then I got a job in
a grocery store deli,

00:04:50.006 --> 00:04:51.797
and then I got a job in a factory,

00:04:51.797 --> 00:04:54.735
sweeping Cheerio dust off the ground.

00:04:54.735 --> 00:04:58.902
And I've never had a job in my
life that I was better than.

00:04:59.905 --> 00:05:02.500
I was always just lucky to have a job,

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:05.683
and every job I had was a
stepping stone to my next job,

00:05:05.683 --> 00:05:09.985
and I never quit my job
until I had my next job.

00:05:09.985 --> 00:05:12.984
And so opportunities look a lot like work.

00:05:12.984 --> 00:05:17.035
But I took this big risk and quit school.

00:05:17.035 --> 00:05:20.202
I quit the one thing that was surefire

00:05:21.251 --> 00:05:23.584
and went and became a model,

00:05:25.157 --> 00:05:27.001
and got really bored really fast,

00:05:27.001 --> 00:05:29.518
and didn't love being a coat hanger.

00:05:29.518 --> 00:05:31.214
And then I became an actor,

00:05:31.214 --> 00:05:34.547
which was like a coat hanger that talks,

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:41.118
and then I ultimately came
full loop and came back around,

00:05:41.118 --> 00:05:42.533
and by the way, when you talk,

00:05:42.533 --> 00:05:44.437
you're generally not even
speaking your own words.

00:05:44.437 --> 00:05:46.554
You're speaking somebody else's words

00:05:46.554 --> 00:05:48.701
and trying to make it sound really real.

00:05:48.701 --> 00:05:51.243
And then I came full loop around

00:05:51.243 --> 00:05:53.704
and started looking at technology again.

00:05:53.704 --> 00:05:55.218
I started getting
interested in technology,

00:05:55.218 --> 00:05:57.436
and started getting
interested in engineering,

00:05:57.436 --> 00:06:01.655
and started really getting
interested in solving problems,

00:06:01.655 --> 00:06:03.688
solving my own problems

00:06:03.688 --> 00:06:05.459
and then solving other people's problems

00:06:05.459 --> 00:06:06.591
and what the value of that is.

00:06:06.591 --> 00:06:10.004
And so whether it's
technology or philanthropy or,

00:06:10.004 --> 00:06:13.911
actually even performing
or being an actor,

00:06:13.911 --> 00:06:16.697
I started to make it
about solving problems.

00:06:16.697 --> 00:06:20.798
And I think that that's probably
the single biggest thing

00:06:20.798 --> 00:06:23.957
that as an angel investor I
look for in entrepreneurs,

00:06:23.957 --> 00:06:27.193
is people who genuinely want to solve

00:06:27.193 --> 00:06:29.482
a problem, a real problem,

00:06:29.482 --> 00:06:31.677
a problem that exists
not just for themselves,

00:06:31.677 --> 00:06:33.716
but sometimes just for themselves

00:06:33.716 --> 00:06:36.470
and then it turns into this wave effect

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:38.729
that solves other people's problems.

00:06:38.729 --> 00:06:41.570
And sometimes by trying
to solve your own problem,

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:44.429
you're solving everybody else's problems.

00:06:44.429 --> 00:06:47.219
'Cause generally, if you
want to affect the world,

00:06:47.219 --> 00:06:49.002
you have to change yourself first.

00:06:49.002 --> 00:06:50.764
Everybody tries to go
out and change the world

00:06:50.764 --> 00:06:52.966
by changing other people and other things,

00:06:52.966 --> 00:06:55.710
but the real way to do it
is by changing yourself,

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:59.199
and sometimes making those
really uncomfortable choices

00:06:59.199 --> 00:07:02.460
to go up against everyone in
your life that you look up to

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:06.245
and take that risk to go be a model.

00:07:06.245 --> 00:07:08.949
What is the most magnificent question?

00:07:08.949 --> 00:07:11.522
Well, I have a question that I like to ask

00:07:11.522 --> 00:07:14.242
to people that I admire in the world.

00:07:14.242 --> 00:07:16.991
And when I run into someone
in the world that I admire,

00:07:16.991 --> 00:07:19.199
and I get to run into these
people quite frequently,

00:07:19.199 --> 00:07:21.547
I like to ask this one
question, and that is,

00:07:21.547 --> 00:07:25.273
"What's the best advice
that you've ever received

00:07:25.273 --> 00:07:28.014
"to get you to where you are?"

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:29.993
And I get amazing answers.

00:07:29.993 --> 00:07:32.953
And I ask this question
in a very particular way.

00:07:32.953 --> 00:07:35.008
I don't ask it when I'm in crisis.

00:07:35.008 --> 00:07:36.953
I don't ask it when I need advice.

00:07:36.953 --> 00:07:39.292
I ask it proactively,
because I sometimes think

00:07:39.292 --> 00:07:41.785
that when you ask people that question of,

00:07:41.785 --> 00:07:44.286
"What is the best advice?"
when you need advice,

00:07:44.286 --> 00:07:46.979
you'll paint their answer
into your question.

00:07:46.979 --> 00:07:48.982
And if you ask them while you're in crisis

00:07:48.982 --> 00:07:50.989
and you expose your crisis to them,

00:07:50.989 --> 00:07:55.678
they'll give you an answer
that is completely colored

00:07:55.678 --> 00:07:58.260
by their own experience
and their own problem,

00:07:58.260 --> 00:07:59.777
which isn't always the best thing to do.

00:07:59.777 --> 00:08:00.998
The best thing to do is build up

00:08:00.998 --> 00:08:05.705
this reservoir, this library,
of extraordinary advice

00:08:05.705 --> 00:08:08.994
that you can access
anytime you're in crisis.

00:08:08.994 --> 00:08:09.942
And that's what I do.

00:08:09.942 --> 00:08:11.069
When I come up against a problem

00:08:11.069 --> 00:08:13.022
or an obstacle or a road block,

00:08:13.022 --> 00:08:15.283
I then go back to my index of advice

00:08:15.283 --> 00:08:17.958
that I've received from
extraordinary people.

00:08:17.958 --> 00:08:19.330
And I look at it.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:23.482
And usually, the answer
is already in there.

00:08:23.482 --> 00:08:25.247
So I thought last night,

00:08:25.247 --> 00:08:28.462
as I was not taking this
opportunity for granted,

00:08:28.462 --> 00:08:30.301
I decided to reach out to some folks

00:08:30.301 --> 00:08:32.238
that I thought you guys might know

00:08:32.238 --> 00:08:34.021
and ask them what the best piece of advice

00:08:34.021 --> 00:08:37.214
they've received was so
that I could share it today.

00:08:37.214 --> 00:08:40.933
So I reached out to my fiance, Mila,

00:08:40.933 --> 00:08:43.018
and that was an easy one, right?

00:08:43.018 --> 00:08:45.269
And she said, "Don't let your ego

00:08:45.269 --> 00:08:48.269
"get in the way of asking for help."

00:08:50.996 --> 00:08:53.041
To me that is great advice,

00:08:53.041 --> 00:08:55.165
and to me, I can use
that advice all the time.

00:08:55.165 --> 00:08:57.983
I reached out to Taylor Swift.

00:08:57.983 --> 00:09:00.982
I figured she's at the top
of the charts right now.

00:09:00.982 --> 00:09:03.926
I wonder if she would
actually even answer my email.

00:09:03.926 --> 00:09:06.254
She did, which is amazing for the person

00:09:06.254 --> 00:09:09.300
who's maybe the most busy
person on the planet right now.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.319
She answered this email because
she thought it was important

00:09:11.319 --> 00:09:13.710
that I share something with you.

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:14.668
And she said,

00:09:14.668 --> 00:09:18.668
"Keep your hopes high and
your expectations low.

00:09:20.228 --> 00:09:22.261
"You're not entitled to anything,

00:09:22.261 --> 00:09:25.936
"but there's nothing that you can't earn."

00:09:25.936 --> 00:09:27.450
I love that one.

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:29.229
You're not entitled to anything,

00:09:29.229 --> 00:09:31.535
but there's nothing that you can't earn.

00:09:31.535 --> 00:09:33.444
"And most importantly,

00:09:33.444 --> 00:09:37.611
"enthusiasm can protect you
from absolutely anything."

00:09:38.776 --> 00:09:40.193
Ooh, that's good.

00:09:41.203 --> 00:09:43.254
When I watch her do interviews,

00:09:43.254 --> 00:09:45.510
now I understand a little
bit more about her,

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:48.951
because she always has that
enthused up, that bubble going.

00:09:48.951 --> 00:09:51.660
And I'm like, "You're
protecting yourself."

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:52.493
I dig it.

00:09:52.493 --> 00:09:53.326
I like it.

00:09:53.326 --> 00:09:54.793
So I reached out to Bradley Cooper.

00:09:54.793 --> 00:09:57.950
He's got one of the number
one movies in America,

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:01.424
this American Sniper movie,
which I think is fantastic.

00:10:01.424 --> 00:10:05.263
And he said, "Hey pal, thanks for asking.

00:10:05.263 --> 00:10:07.508
"You know, often I thought about

00:10:07.508 --> 00:10:11.491
"this old cartoon
commercial when I was a kid

00:10:11.491 --> 00:10:14.765
"that sent a message to be yourself

00:10:14.765 --> 00:10:16.793
"and just how vital that is to growth.

00:10:16.793 --> 00:10:18.518
"And when I finally realized that notion

00:10:18.518 --> 00:10:20.783
"some 30 years after
watching those commercials

00:10:20.783 --> 00:10:22.488
"in between Saturday cartoons,

00:10:22.488 --> 00:10:25.950
"I found a true difference
in my daily life.

00:10:25.950 --> 00:10:28.838
"Knowing that we all have ourselves

00:10:28.838 --> 00:10:32.421
"and all we have
ourselves, our uniqueness,

00:10:33.673 --> 00:10:37.814
"that is what we can use to
cultivate in order to create

00:10:37.814 --> 00:10:42.715
"or do whatever it is that
we want to do in this life."

00:10:42.715 --> 00:10:44.186
Just be yourself.

00:10:44.186 --> 00:10:46.973
Simple advice, but it's
really good advice,

00:10:46.973 --> 00:10:49.266
and I can imagine that advice coming handy

00:10:49.266 --> 00:10:50.680
in a moment of crisis.

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:54.597
So I reached out to Chris
Rock, and I love his.

00:10:57.064 --> 00:10:57.897
"Advice.

00:10:57.897 --> 00:10:59.462
"That's a hard one.

00:10:59.462 --> 00:11:03.629
"How about, one, people that
can't listen can't lead.

00:11:05.202 --> 00:11:08.420
"Two, one of the keys to success

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:11.736
"is the ability to listen people

00:11:11.736 --> 00:11:14.772
"that you know that you are smarter than."

00:11:14.772 --> 00:11:16.741
And I read that and I was like, "Oh, man.

00:11:16.741 --> 00:11:19.190
"Oh no, the whole thesis
of my entire thing,"

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:21.168
which is there are no dumb questions,

00:11:21.168 --> 00:11:23.508
but there are dumb questions,

00:11:23.508 --> 00:11:26.234
maybe I just undermined myself
with that piece of advice.

00:11:26.234 --> 00:11:27.936
And I do think that that's important.

00:11:27.936 --> 00:11:30.196
I do think that it's
important to listen to people

00:11:30.196 --> 00:11:31.710
that you know that you're smarter than,

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:34.017
'cause you may not be.

00:11:34.017 --> 00:11:36.414
And then he signed it,
"The CEO of nothing,"

00:11:36.414 --> 00:11:38.010
which I thought was pretty good.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:39.690
And then I reached out to Eric Schmidt,

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:41.650
who's a friend of mine, and he said,

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:43.249
"Always return phone calls.

00:11:43.249 --> 00:11:44.405
"Take the time.

00:11:44.405 --> 00:11:47.298
"If you put good energy
out, it comes back.

00:11:47.298 --> 00:11:49.631
"Whenever possible, say yes.

00:11:51.003 --> 00:11:53.425
"Life is just who you travel with."

00:11:53.425 --> 00:11:54.975
I thought that was pretty killer.

00:11:54.975 --> 00:11:57.473
And this list goes on and on and on.

00:11:57.473 --> 00:11:59.652
And what I found is, most
successful people always have

00:11:59.652 --> 00:12:01.205
more than one piece of advice.

00:12:01.205 --> 00:12:04.659
(audience laughs)

00:12:04.659 --> 00:12:05.775
That's my question.

00:12:05.775 --> 00:12:07.543
That's the question that I like to ask,

00:12:07.543 --> 00:12:09.656
and that's the question
that leads me to solutions

00:12:09.656 --> 00:12:11.445
and leads me to road blocks sometimes

00:12:11.445 --> 00:12:14.994
and leads me to internal quandary,

00:12:14.994 --> 00:12:18.183
which leads me to be a
better person, I think.

00:12:18.183 --> 00:12:21.266
The sexiest thing in the entire world

00:12:22.476 --> 00:12:24.421
(audience cheering)

00:12:24.421 --> 00:12:26.026
is being really smart.

00:12:26.026 --> 00:12:29.026
(audience cheering)

00:12:30.474 --> 00:12:34.150
And being thoughtful, and being generous.

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:35.677
(audience cheering)

00:12:35.677 --> 00:12:39.476
Everything else is crap, I promise you.

00:12:39.476 --> 00:12:42.455
(audience cheering)

00:12:42.455 --> 00:12:46.455
It's just crap that
people try to sell to you

00:12:46.455 --> 00:12:50.251
to make you feel like
less, so don't buy it.

00:12:50.251 --> 00:12:51.512
(audience cheering)

00:12:51.512 --> 00:12:55.402
Be smart, be thoughtful, and be generous.

00:12:55.402 --> 00:12:56.875
I like to sit down with founders

00:12:56.875 --> 00:13:00.486
and I like to work on removing
friction from process,

00:13:00.486 --> 00:13:02.206
whatever it might be,

00:13:02.206 --> 00:13:04.202
and creating products that are intuitive.

00:13:04.202 --> 00:13:05.929
And one of the things that I get

00:13:05.929 --> 00:13:08.959
from working in the creative
space and being an actor

00:13:08.959 --> 00:13:13.471
is really understanding
feeling and touch and art,

00:13:13.471 --> 00:13:15.600
and it's an art.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:18.736
Being able to create in impulse

00:13:18.736 --> 00:13:22.247
within a consumer to do
something is an art form.

00:13:22.247 --> 00:13:24.453
I can probably give you 10
or 20 examples of companies

00:13:24.453 --> 00:13:26.280
that have really just taken

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:29.089
software that was archaic software

00:13:29.089 --> 00:13:31.420
that was untouchable by consumers

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:35.149
and actually brought it
down to a consumer level.

00:13:35.149 --> 00:13:37.386
That is becoming the process

00:13:37.386 --> 00:13:39.779
to building a great
software company these days.

00:13:39.779 --> 00:13:42.236
You can look at a company like Zenefits.

00:13:42.236 --> 00:13:43.986
There's always been HR software

00:13:43.986 --> 00:13:46.183
that's been out there in
some way, shape or form.

00:13:46.183 --> 00:13:47.900
Or you can look at Robinhood,

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:50.438
and there's always been
ways that you can interface

00:13:50.438 --> 00:13:52.407
and do stock exchanges.

00:13:52.407 --> 00:13:54.876
But making it something that

00:13:54.876 --> 00:13:58.024
the average consumer can
intuitively understand

00:13:58.024 --> 00:14:00.433
and touch and play with and use

00:14:00.433 --> 00:14:03.043
and have it be beneficial in their life

00:14:03.043 --> 00:14:05.521
is about understanding the intuition

00:14:05.521 --> 00:14:07.560
and the feeling of the consumer.

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:09.965
Generosity has to be at the core

00:14:09.965 --> 00:14:11.443
of anything and everything we do.

00:14:11.443 --> 00:14:13.193
Some of the best advice I ever received is

00:14:13.193 --> 00:14:15.314
somebody once told me that

00:14:15.314 --> 00:14:18.224
the difference between good
and bad is very simple.

00:14:18.224 --> 00:14:20.934
Is it good for you, is it good for me,

00:14:20.934 --> 00:14:22.457
and is it good for the greater good?

00:14:22.457 --> 00:14:26.760
If it doesn't fall into those
three categories, it is bad.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:29.536
Is it good for you, is it good for me,

00:14:29.536 --> 00:14:31.040
and is it good for the greater good?

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:34.997
And if our actions are oriented
by actions of generosity

00:14:34.997 --> 00:14:36.684
that are good for me and good for you

00:14:36.684 --> 00:14:37.963
and good for the greater good,

00:14:37.963 --> 00:14:40.232
we'll always be moving the boat forward,

00:14:40.232 --> 00:14:42.955
the boat that we're all in.

00:14:42.955 --> 00:14:44.947
I think generosity is paramount.

00:14:44.947 --> 00:14:47.962
Steve Jobs said when you grow up,

00:14:47.962 --> 00:14:51.987
you tend to get told that the
world is the way that it is,

00:14:51.987 --> 00:14:55.790
and that your life is to live
your life inside the world

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:57.969
and try not to get in too much trouble,

00:14:57.969 --> 00:15:00.691
and maybe get an education and get a job

00:15:00.691 --> 00:15:03.492
and make some money and have a family.

00:15:03.492 --> 00:15:05.520
But life can be a lot broader than that

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:08.529
when you realize one simple thing,

00:15:08.529 --> 00:15:13.397
and that is that everything
around us that we call life

00:15:13.397 --> 00:15:16.730
was made up by people that
are no smarter than you.

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:20.439
(audience cheering)

00:15:20.439 --> 00:15:22.505
And you can build your own things.

00:15:22.505 --> 00:15:26.570
You can build your own life
that other people can live in.

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:27.903
So build a life.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:30.765
Don't live one, build one.

00:15:30.765 --> 00:15:33.229
I see a lot of entrepreneurs that come in,

00:15:33.229 --> 00:15:37.512
and they have a really great
idea to solve something.

00:15:37.512 --> 00:15:38.522
And I start to talk to them.

00:15:38.522 --> 00:15:42.464
I start to find out who they
are and what they're about.

00:15:42.464 --> 00:15:46.589
And they sort of jump from the
problem that they're solving

00:15:46.589 --> 00:15:50.756
to who they're going to be
or what they're going to get.

00:15:51.921 --> 00:15:55.446
It's like I have this red flag
in my pocket that goes up,

00:15:55.446 --> 00:15:57.029
and I'm like, "Wow.

00:15:57.955 --> 00:16:01.742
"That guy wants to be Mark Zuckerberg.

00:16:01.742 --> 00:16:04.159
"That guy wants to be Pinkus.

00:16:05.401 --> 00:16:08.228
That guy wants to be Steve Jobs."

00:16:08.228 --> 00:16:10.721
Or that guy just has got
this idea, and he's like,

00:16:10.721 --> 00:16:12.365
"Yeah, and the market cap
is this and it's this,

00:16:12.365 --> 00:16:13.528
"and we're going to make this much money

00:16:13.528 --> 00:16:16.469
"and it's going to be a
billion dollar company."

00:16:16.469 --> 00:16:18.925
And then I have this switch in my head

00:16:18.925 --> 00:16:22.926
that just starts to shut off
really slowly when I hear this,

00:16:22.926 --> 00:16:26.431
because they're jumping to the effect.

00:16:26.431 --> 00:16:29.921
And if you want to be a real entrepreneur,

00:16:29.921 --> 00:16:31.489
you have to be the cause.

00:16:31.489 --> 00:16:36.245
You have to be the creator of
somebody else's new reality,

00:16:36.245 --> 00:16:41.218
which eliminates time, space,
motion, pain, friction.

00:16:41.218 --> 00:16:42.961
That's what engineering is.

00:16:42.961 --> 00:16:44.221
It was the first thing

00:16:44.221 --> 00:16:45.560
that I learned as an engineering student,

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:48.143
was scientists look at problems

00:16:49.930 --> 00:16:53.673
and they find out about problems,
and engineers solve them.

00:16:53.673 --> 00:16:58.449
And it's the one thing that
really, really stuck with me.

00:16:58.449 --> 00:17:02.505
And those guys that want
to be Mark Zuckerberg,

00:17:02.505 --> 00:17:06.588
or girls that want to be
the next big entrepreneur,

00:17:07.457 --> 00:17:11.244
not the next big, but the
one that already exists,

00:17:11.244 --> 00:17:13.686
if you want to be Mark Zuckerberg,

00:17:13.686 --> 00:17:17.005
the best you're ever going
to be is second place,

00:17:17.005 --> 00:17:19.185
'cause Mark Zuckerberg will always be

00:17:19.185 --> 00:17:20.927
a better Mark Zuckerberg than you.

00:17:20.927 --> 00:17:22.974
(audience laughs)

00:17:22.974 --> 00:17:25.799
(audience applauds)

00:17:25.799 --> 00:17:29.511
And if your chase is for
a billion dollar company,

00:17:29.511 --> 00:17:31.678
you may not make a dollar.

00:17:32.664 --> 00:17:34.303
So I wanted to tell this story

00:17:34.303 --> 00:17:36.803
about a guy named Carl Fisher.

00:17:38.501 --> 00:17:41.512
How many people here
know who Carl Fisher is?

00:17:41.512 --> 00:17:42.845
A couple people.

00:17:43.965 --> 00:17:46.534
It's interesting only a couple
people know who this guy is,

00:17:46.534 --> 00:17:48.955
and after you hear this
story, you're going to wonder,

00:17:48.955 --> 00:17:51.207
"Wow, how do I not know who this guy is?"

00:17:51.207 --> 00:17:53.690
And when I heard this story...

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:55.966
Carl Fisher was one of
the greatest marketers

00:17:55.966 --> 00:17:57.633
in American history.

00:17:58.524 --> 00:18:02.155
As a young kid, Carl Fisher
couldn't see very well.

00:18:02.155 --> 00:18:04.926
His eyesight was pretty poor,
and he would go to school

00:18:04.926 --> 00:18:08.174
and he would sit in the front
of the class trying to see,

00:18:08.174 --> 00:18:09.882
but he was still a poor student.

00:18:09.882 --> 00:18:10.715
He didn't do well,

00:18:10.715 --> 00:18:11.979
and the teacher moved him
to the back of the class.

00:18:11.979 --> 00:18:14.216
So when he was a young kid, he quit school

00:18:14.216 --> 00:18:15.470
because there was nothing
really there for him.

00:18:15.470 --> 00:18:17.676
He couldn't even see the damn blackboard.

00:18:17.676 --> 00:18:20.728
So he was like, "Fuck
it, I'm out of here."

00:18:20.728 --> 00:18:22.199
He bails.

00:18:22.199 --> 00:18:24.532
So then Carl gets this idea,

00:18:26.269 --> 00:18:29.413
and he's going to sell candy on the train.

00:18:29.413 --> 00:18:30.495
There was this candy boys,

00:18:30.495 --> 00:18:32.612
and they walked down the
aisle with these boxes,

00:18:32.612 --> 00:18:33.570
and they'd sell candy.

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:34.726
And so Carl gets this idea.

00:18:34.726 --> 00:18:36.987
But Carl is no ordinary Carl.

00:18:36.987 --> 00:18:39.320
Carl is an amazing marketer.

00:18:40.693 --> 00:18:44.757
And so what he does is he gets a t-shirt,

00:18:44.757 --> 00:18:49.418
and on the t-shirt he pins
this print of a naked lady.

00:18:49.418 --> 00:18:50.272
(audience laughs)

00:18:50.272 --> 00:18:53.269
And this shmock, smock, whatever
you call a Jewish smock.

00:18:53.269 --> 00:18:55.000
It's shmock in Jewish.

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:56.544
It's Shabbat, so I can say shmock.

00:18:56.544 --> 00:18:58.157
He has a shmock.

00:18:58.157 --> 00:19:00.963
And he walks down the
aisle with his candy box,

00:19:00.963 --> 00:19:03.222
and as he passes these
businessmen, he goes like this,

00:19:03.222 --> 00:19:04.636
and keeps walking.

00:19:04.636 --> 00:19:06.236
And they're like, "Hey,
kid, get back here."

00:19:06.236 --> 00:19:07.945
(audience laughs)

00:19:07.945 --> 00:19:10.228
Carl says, "Okay."

00:19:10.228 --> 00:19:11.282
They would be like, "What's on that?

00:19:11.282 --> 00:19:12.307
"What do you got underneath there?"

00:19:12.307 --> 00:19:15.003
And he's like, "I'll show
you what's underneath there.

00:19:15.003 --> 00:19:16.538
"Just buy some candy."

00:19:16.538 --> 00:19:17.793
(audience laughs)

00:19:17.793 --> 00:19:20.208
Phenomenal marketing tactic, right?

00:19:20.208 --> 00:19:22.756
Like I'm going to show you
a little bit of the juice,

00:19:22.756 --> 00:19:23.991
and then I'm going to shut it off,

00:19:23.991 --> 00:19:25.468
and then once you buy this thing,

00:19:25.468 --> 00:19:27.975
I'm going to give you the full product.

00:19:27.975 --> 00:19:30.281
And so Carl makes a lot of money,

00:19:30.281 --> 00:19:33.466
'cause these guys really
want to see naked pictures.

00:19:33.466 --> 00:19:34.447
(audience laughs)

00:19:34.447 --> 00:19:37.238
We all know that you want
women on your platform early,

00:19:37.238 --> 00:19:39.950
hot ones first, and then everybody comes.

00:19:39.950 --> 00:19:42.867
Carl knew this way back in the day.

00:19:45.013 --> 00:19:46.754
So he makes a lot of money,

00:19:46.754 --> 00:19:50.264
and then he gets this idea
he wants to sell bicycles.

00:19:50.264 --> 00:19:52.233
So Carl Fisher, he sees
all these bicycles.

00:19:52.233 --> 00:19:54.483
This is back in late 1800s.

00:19:55.682 --> 00:19:58.200
He sees all these bicycles
in Europe, and he's thinking,

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:00.199
"This is going to explode
in the United States."

00:20:00.199 --> 00:20:02.758
And so he opens up a bicycle shop.

00:20:02.758 --> 00:20:03.664
And nobody comes.

00:20:03.664 --> 00:20:05.012
Nobody is buying bicycles.

00:20:05.012 --> 00:20:07.993
And so he goes and he gets this guy.

00:20:07.993 --> 00:20:11.660
He puts a wire from one
building to the next building,

00:20:11.660 --> 00:20:12.985
and he gets a guy that knows

00:20:12.985 --> 00:20:15.286
how to tightrope on the bicycle.

00:20:15.286 --> 00:20:17.516
And people come and they're
like, "Wow, this is amazing."

00:20:17.516 --> 00:20:19.476
And he finds some guy that
can do a trick on a bicycle,

00:20:19.476 --> 00:20:21.753
and people start coming
and buying bicycles.

00:20:21.753 --> 00:20:22.688
And so he starts to sell

00:20:22.688 --> 00:20:25.168
these bicycles and sell these bicycles.

00:20:25.168 --> 00:20:27.763
And then the automobile starts to boom.

00:20:27.763 --> 00:20:30.514
And he's like, "Wow, this is the future."

00:20:30.514 --> 00:20:32.895
Now you got to remember,
Carl can't see so well,

00:20:32.895 --> 00:20:35.744
but he gets a car with all
his money, and he loves it.

00:20:35.744 --> 00:20:38.209
And he's driving this car, blind as a bat,

00:20:38.209 --> 00:20:40.764
and he loves the speed of this thing,

00:20:40.764 --> 00:20:43.246
and the agility and the
control that he has.

00:20:43.246 --> 00:20:46.779
And so he opens up a car
shop, and he opened up

00:20:46.779 --> 00:20:50.460
basically the first automotive
dealership in the world.

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:52.553
And so he starts selling these
cars and selling these cars

00:20:52.553 --> 00:20:54.492
and making a lot of
money and a lot of money,

00:20:54.492 --> 00:20:56.711
and sells his bike shop, obviously,

00:20:56.711 --> 00:20:57.994
in order to do the down payment.

00:20:57.994 --> 00:20:59.912
Takes this bigger risk with cars,

00:20:59.912 --> 00:21:01.689
and of course, people start buying cars,

00:21:01.689 --> 00:21:03.530
and it's the first automobile dealership

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:05.475
in the United States.

00:21:05.475 --> 00:21:06.987
And then he starts thinking to himself.

00:21:06.987 --> 00:21:08.256
He's seen all these people driving cars,

00:21:08.256 --> 00:21:10.428
and he thinks, "Someday, these people

00:21:10.428 --> 00:21:13.783
"are going to want to
drive these cars at night."

00:21:13.783 --> 00:21:16.728
And so he invents the headlight.

00:21:16.728 --> 00:21:20.437
And he sells the headlight,
and he makes a lot of money.

00:21:20.437 --> 00:21:22.720
And so now he's a millionaire

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:25.228
back at the turn of the century,

00:21:25.228 --> 00:21:27.487
and he's got all this money,

00:21:27.487 --> 00:21:29.984
and he's doing extremely well for himself.

00:21:29.984 --> 00:21:34.631
And he goes to, I think
he was in Minneapolis,

00:21:34.631 --> 00:21:37.271
and he's trying to sell cars
and sell this headlight.

00:21:37.271 --> 00:21:40.024
So he hooks this car
up to a hot air balloon

00:21:40.024 --> 00:21:43.901
and he's flying over
the city in Minneapolis

00:21:43.901 --> 00:21:46.474
and trying to sell these
cars and promote his brand,

00:21:46.474 --> 00:21:47.777
promote what he has.

00:21:47.777 --> 00:21:50.965
And he sees this girl
down in the crowd below,

00:21:50.965 --> 00:21:52.468
young girl, beautiful girl.

00:21:52.468 --> 00:21:53.579
And he says, "Land the balloon.

00:21:53.579 --> 00:21:55.260
"I have to go meet this girl."

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:58.016
So he meets the girl, they fall in love.

00:21:58.016 --> 00:21:59.685
And she's like, "You've worked so hard.

00:21:59.685 --> 00:22:00.518
"You have all this money.

00:22:00.518 --> 00:22:01.940
"Why do you keep working?

00:22:01.940 --> 00:22:03.089
"What are you trying to do?"

00:22:03.089 --> 00:22:05.467
And Carl is like, "I just see things."

00:22:05.467 --> 00:22:08.771
He was blind, blind as a
bat, but he had vision.

00:22:08.771 --> 00:22:10.450
And he was like, "I just see these things,

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:11.298
"and I have to make them happen."

00:22:11.298 --> 00:22:12.256
And she's like, "Well, you should

00:22:12.256 --> 00:22:14.460
"just really settle
down and have a family."

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:17.252
And he's like, "Yeah, but
this is what I want to do."

00:22:17.252 --> 00:22:20.219
And he's like, "Well, I'm just
going to do one more thing,

00:22:20.219 --> 00:22:21.912
"and then I'll stop."

00:22:21.912 --> 00:22:24.980
And so he goes to Indianapolis,

00:22:24.980 --> 00:22:27.535
and he has this idea that
these people love cars.

00:22:27.535 --> 00:22:29.771
(mumbles) obsessed with cars.

00:22:29.771 --> 00:22:31.937
He's like, "I think people
will want to race these cars.

00:22:31.937 --> 00:22:33.214
"They're already racing them."

00:22:33.214 --> 00:22:35.147
So he's going to make this track,

00:22:35.147 --> 00:22:36.526
and people are going to race these cars.

00:22:36.526 --> 00:22:37.754
So he invents the Indy 500.

00:22:37.754 --> 00:22:39.458
He builds the Brickyard,

00:22:39.458 --> 00:22:41.185
and people are like, "Carl, you're crazy.

00:22:41.185 --> 00:22:44.471
"Nobody is going to watch
cars go around in a circle.

00:22:44.471 --> 00:22:45.455
"This is insane."

00:22:45.455 --> 00:22:47.526
(audience laughs)

00:22:47.526 --> 00:22:50.683
And he says, "Not only
will they come and watch,

00:22:50.683 --> 00:22:52.455
"they'll pay a dollar."

00:22:52.455 --> 00:22:53.663
And so he starts to charge people,

00:22:53.663 --> 00:22:55.031
and he invents the Indy 500.

00:22:55.031 --> 00:22:56.247
He invents the Brickyard.

00:22:56.247 --> 00:22:59.009
And the first race, like six
people die, because people--

00:22:59.009 --> 00:22:59.953
(audience laughs)

00:22:59.953 --> 00:23:00.786
Really funny.

00:23:00.786 --> 00:23:02.645
That guy just died, guys.

00:23:02.645 --> 00:23:03.728
Slow it down.

00:23:05.461 --> 00:23:06.294
Too soon.

00:23:06.294 --> 00:23:07.127
That's too soon?

00:23:07.127 --> 00:23:07.960
Okay.

00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:09.921
So anyway, like six people
die in the first race

00:23:09.921 --> 00:23:12.950
'cause it's the brick and
people are flying off.

00:23:12.950 --> 00:23:15.680
And so it was a huge disaster.

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:17.759
But then he stays at
it and he stays at it,

00:23:17.759 --> 00:23:19.735
'cause even though he failed,

00:23:19.735 --> 00:23:20.794
he knew that he could change it.

00:23:20.794 --> 00:23:22.648
And he changed the surface,
and people start to come,

00:23:22.648 --> 00:23:23.951
and it actually starts to grow,

00:23:23.951 --> 00:23:26.757
and it builds into the Indy 500 as it is.

00:23:26.757 --> 00:23:30.764
His wife is like, "Okay,
Carl. this is a debacle.

00:23:30.764 --> 00:23:31.959
"Let's just stop doing these things.

00:23:31.959 --> 00:23:33.016
"Let's go on vacation."

00:23:33.016 --> 00:23:34.666
So he takes 'em on vacation.

00:23:34.666 --> 00:23:36.531
This is around like 1910.

00:23:36.531 --> 00:23:40.698
They go down to Florida, which
at the time was like this,

00:23:41.888 --> 00:23:44.489
there was a couple little resorty areas,

00:23:44.489 --> 00:23:48.523
but it was kind of a mosquito
infested environment.

00:23:48.523 --> 00:23:50.211
But they're there and they're on vacation,

00:23:50.211 --> 00:23:54.253
and he sees this bridge
going out into the distance,

00:23:54.253 --> 00:23:55.739
and he's like, "What the hell is that?"

00:23:55.739 --> 00:23:57.010
And the wife is like, "Carl, don't.

00:23:57.010 --> 00:23:57.843
"Don't go.

00:23:57.843 --> 00:23:58.962
(audience laughs)

00:23:58.962 --> 00:23:59.989
"Just relax."

00:23:59.989 --> 00:24:01.777
And he's like, "I just want to find out

00:24:01.777 --> 00:24:03.018
"what's going on with this thing."

00:24:03.018 --> 00:24:04.091
So he goes and he hunts down the guy,

00:24:04.091 --> 00:24:05.989
and he finds out this
guy is trying to build

00:24:05.989 --> 00:24:08.003
the largest bridge in the world.

00:24:08.003 --> 00:24:11.488
And Carl is like, "Well,
what's on the other side?"

00:24:11.488 --> 00:24:12.818
And the guy is like, "Well, nothing.

00:24:12.818 --> 00:24:14.490
"It's just a swamp area.

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:15.573
"It's nothing."

00:24:15.573 --> 00:24:16.668
(audience laughs)

00:24:16.668 --> 00:24:19.695
And so Carl says, "Well,
take me over there."

00:24:19.695 --> 00:24:22.950
So they go over, and he
starts looking around,

00:24:22.950 --> 00:24:25.280
and he's surveying the
land, and he's looking.

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:27.924
He's like, "Wait a second."

00:24:27.924 --> 00:24:29.945
And he sort of gets down
on his hands and knees,

00:24:29.945 --> 00:24:32.244
and he's looking and
drawing a little thing.

00:24:32.244 --> 00:24:33.249
And the guy is like, "What are you doing?"

00:24:33.249 --> 00:24:36.756
He's like, "I think we
could backfill all this

00:24:36.756 --> 00:24:40.089
"and turn it into a giant resort place."

00:24:42.261 --> 00:24:43.454
And the guy is like,

00:24:43.454 --> 00:24:44.504
"Well, if you want to
do it, you can do it.

00:24:44.504 --> 00:24:46.275
"I'm not getting involved."

00:24:46.275 --> 00:24:47.952
And he's like, "Well, yeah,

00:24:47.952 --> 00:24:50.384
"but you want people to come
for your bridge, right?"

00:24:50.384 --> 00:24:51.790
Because the guy couldn't
get money, he's like,

00:24:51.790 --> 00:24:53.985
"I'll help you finish the bridge.

00:24:53.985 --> 00:24:56.481
"I want the land on the other side."

00:24:56.481 --> 00:24:58.740
And the guy is like, "Okay."

00:24:58.740 --> 00:25:00.707
So he starts backfilling,
backfilling, backfilling,

00:25:00.707 --> 00:25:03.146
builds it up, builds
up these giant casinos,

00:25:03.146 --> 00:25:04.668
does this whole thing.

00:25:04.668 --> 00:25:06.740
It's Miami Beach.

00:25:06.740 --> 00:25:08.907
He's building Miami Beach.

00:25:10.258 --> 00:25:13.973
Carl is otherwise known as
Mr. Miami because of this.

00:25:13.973 --> 00:25:15.945
And he fills it in and he
builds all these casinos

00:25:15.945 --> 00:25:18.498
and this huge area and this whole thing.

00:25:18.498 --> 00:25:19.915
And nobody comes.

00:25:21.002 --> 00:25:22.973
And he's got this giant
infrastructure now,

00:25:22.973 --> 00:25:26.973
casinos and stores and
houses, and nobody comes.

00:25:29.033 --> 00:25:30.783
And he's like, "Oh man,
I got to do something."

00:25:30.783 --> 00:25:32.653
So he gets an elephant.

00:25:32.653 --> 00:25:34.708
People in America hadn't seen an elephant.

00:25:34.708 --> 00:25:35.541
But if there's an elephant,

00:25:35.541 --> 00:25:36.490
people will come to see the elephant.

00:25:36.490 --> 00:25:37.543
And he starts to tell people.

00:25:37.543 --> 00:25:41.917
He goes to Congress and
petitions for a highway

00:25:41.917 --> 00:25:44.280
from the north coming south,
and they build the Dixie Lane.

00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:45.276
He had enough money at the time

00:25:45.276 --> 00:25:47.758
where he could convince them
to build the Dixie Lane Highway

00:25:47.758 --> 00:25:51.208
from all the way up north
all the way down to Miami.

00:25:51.208 --> 00:25:53.181
And nobody comes.

00:25:53.181 --> 00:25:55.170
And he's like, "This is a nightmare."

00:25:55.170 --> 00:25:56.753
He's dumped everything he had,

00:25:56.753 --> 00:25:58.540
all the money from the
headlight, into this thing,

00:25:58.540 --> 00:25:59.957
and nobody comes.

00:26:01.004 --> 00:26:04.220
And so he kidnaps the
president of the United States,

00:26:04.220 --> 00:26:05.711
and he takes the president
of the United States

00:26:05.711 --> 00:26:08.703
out into this place and gets
photographs of him there.

00:26:08.703 --> 00:26:10.688
He's friends with him, so
he sort of kidnapped him.

00:26:10.688 --> 00:26:12.931
It's a better story if he
actually kidnapped him,

00:26:12.931 --> 00:26:14.698
but I made that part up.

00:26:14.698 --> 00:26:16.263
(audience laughs)

00:26:16.263 --> 00:26:17.687
So he takes the president
of the United States

00:26:17.687 --> 00:26:21.661
and he takes pictures of
him out in this island,

00:26:21.661 --> 00:26:25.328
and people start to
come, one by one by one,

00:26:26.463 --> 00:26:29.757
and it's the biggest land
boom in American history.

00:26:29.757 --> 00:26:30.944
And there are people who are literally

00:26:30.944 --> 00:26:33.956
setting up card tables in
their front lawn, selling land,

00:26:33.956 --> 00:26:35.295
and it's going, and it's going.

00:26:35.295 --> 00:26:37.298
It's like the Internet right now.

00:26:37.298 --> 00:26:38.993
It's just happening,
and everybody is coming,

00:26:38.993 --> 00:26:40.253
and they're coming and they're coming,

00:26:40.253 --> 00:26:41.724
and they're selling, and
the prices are going up,

00:26:41.724 --> 00:26:43.261
and Carl is making millions.

00:26:43.261 --> 00:26:46.094
He makes $100 million before 1920.

00:26:48.447 --> 00:26:50.991
And then he gets a brilliant idea.

00:26:50.991 --> 00:26:53.420
(audience laughs)

00:26:53.420 --> 00:26:55.753
Montauk, Miami of the north.

00:26:59.253 --> 00:27:01.959
And he dumps it all into Montauk.

00:27:01.959 --> 00:27:04.484
Everything he's got goes into Montauk.

00:27:04.484 --> 00:27:05.476
And he's building it up.

00:27:05.476 --> 00:27:06.990
His wife is furious.

00:27:06.990 --> 00:27:09.157
By the way, she adopted a baby
he didn't even know about.

00:27:09.157 --> 00:27:10.953
That's a whole nightmare story.

00:27:10.953 --> 00:27:13.726
Carl starts drinking a lot.

00:27:13.726 --> 00:27:15.209
Builds up Montauk.

00:27:15.209 --> 00:27:16.182
The stock market crashes.

00:27:16.182 --> 00:27:17.736
He loses everything.

00:27:17.736 --> 00:27:19.486
Completely wiped out.

00:27:20.481 --> 00:27:24.648
Carl Fisher died in a trailer
home outside of Miami,

00:27:25.677 --> 00:27:29.096
and about a week before he died,

00:27:29.096 --> 00:27:31.252
somebody took him up in a blimp

00:27:31.252 --> 00:27:33.187
and they took him over Miami,

00:27:33.187 --> 00:27:36.903
and he was able to see what he had built.

00:27:36.903 --> 00:27:38.751
But that's all he had.

00:27:38.751 --> 00:27:40.488
He didn't have a dime.

00:27:40.488 --> 00:27:42.491
He didn't have a penny.

00:27:42.491 --> 00:27:45.658
Hardly anyone would remember his name.

00:27:47.502 --> 00:27:50.425
But he gave that to all of those people.

00:27:50.425 --> 00:27:53.659
And he knew that no one could
ever take that away from him.

00:27:53.659 --> 00:27:55.742
He could never lose that.

00:27:57.181 --> 00:27:59.419
He might not have been able to see,

00:27:59.419 --> 00:28:02.222
but he had the vision to know

00:28:02.222 --> 00:28:06.010
that it wasn't about his name
or somebody remembering him.

00:28:06.010 --> 00:28:08.486
It wasn't about the billion dollars.

00:28:08.486 --> 00:28:13.155
It was about uncovering
that which was concealed,

00:28:13.155 --> 00:28:15.721
the swampland, the corners of the room,

00:28:15.721 --> 00:28:17.094
the way people can exchange,

00:28:17.094 --> 00:28:19.219
the way people can
connect with each other,

00:28:19.219 --> 00:28:21.991
the way that a database can be built.

00:28:21.991 --> 00:28:23.678
It's about uncovering.

00:28:23.678 --> 00:28:26.291
It's about Airbnb changing
the hotel industry.

00:28:26.291 --> 00:28:29.958
It's about disrupting markets boldly

00:28:29.958 --> 00:28:32.482
in a way that you can leave that behind

00:28:32.482 --> 00:28:35.979
for everybody else to have and share.

00:28:35.979 --> 00:28:38.414
And Carl Fisher knew that.

00:28:38.414 --> 00:28:40.766
So you might not remember his name,

00:28:40.766 --> 00:28:43.227
but you use his product every day.

00:28:43.227 --> 00:28:45.942
You might've visited his town.

00:28:45.942 --> 00:28:50.777
And the only thing I can
impart upon young entrepreneurs

00:28:50.777 --> 00:28:54.261
is it's not about being
like somebody else.

00:28:54.261 --> 00:28:56.521
It's not about the billion dollars.

00:28:56.521 --> 00:28:59.770
It's about how you can
affect other people's lives,

00:28:59.770 --> 00:29:01.663
enrich them, improve them,

00:29:01.663 --> 00:29:05.167
how you can eliminate
the space between people,

00:29:05.167 --> 00:29:07.741
how you can eliminate pain and friction,

00:29:07.741 --> 00:29:10.791
and how you can uncover that which one day

00:29:10.791 --> 00:29:12.874
was just a big swampland.

00:29:16.244 --> 00:29:17.848
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:29:17.848 --> 00:29:20.736
I made this video because
Kevin Lopez asked me to.

00:29:20.736 --> 00:29:21.944
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:29:21.944 --> 00:29:23.516
that you want me to profile next,

00:29:23.516 --> 00:29:24.895
leave it down in the comments below

00:29:24.895 --> 00:29:25.902
and I'll see what I can do.

00:29:25.902 --> 00:29:26.860
I'd also love to know,

00:29:26.860 --> 00:29:29.245
what did Ashton say that
really resonated with you?

00:29:29.245 --> 00:29:31.143
What was the most
important take home point

00:29:31.143 --> 00:29:32.701
that you took from this video?

00:29:32.701 --> 00:29:33.731
Leave it in the comments.

00:29:33.731 --> 00:29:35.274
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:29:35.274 --> 00:29:36.765
Thank you again for watching.

00:29:36.765 --> 00:29:38.152
I believe in you.

00:29:38.152 --> 00:29:39.974
I hope you believe in yourself

00:29:39.974 --> 00:29:42.212
and whatever your one word is.

00:29:42.212 --> 00:29:43.461
I'll see you soon.

00:29:43.461 --> 00:29:45.961
(loud whoosh)

00:29:49.760 --> 00:29:52.427
(phone ringing)

00:29:53.693 --> 00:29:54.526
- Yeah.

00:29:54.526 --> 00:29:56.422
- [Mike] Hey Steve, it's Mike.

00:29:56.422 --> 00:29:57.255
- Hey.

00:29:57.255 --> 00:30:00.456
- [Mike] Hey, I think I may
have found a project for you.

00:30:00.456 --> 00:30:02.469
It's more of an R and D
outcast than anything,

00:30:02.469 --> 00:30:04.751
but it's actually a project
you approved some time back.

00:30:04.751 --> 00:30:07.001
Why don't you look into it?

00:30:12.651 --> 00:30:14.981
- So this is the Macintosh team?

00:30:14.981 --> 00:30:15.814
- Mm-hmm.

00:30:16.745 --> 00:30:17.713
Jesus.

00:30:17.713 --> 00:30:19.546
- No, it's just Steve.

00:30:20.554 --> 00:30:22.665
- It's really good to see you, Steve.

00:30:22.665 --> 00:30:24.589
- Bill (mumbles)

00:30:24.589 --> 00:30:26.158
It's good to see they
got A players over here.

00:30:26.158 --> 00:30:26.991
- Mm-hmm.

00:30:32.991 --> 00:30:34.669
- Hi, Steve Jobs.

00:30:34.669 --> 00:30:35.792
- What's your name?

00:30:35.792 --> 00:30:37.707
- (mumbles) (clears throat) Burrell Smith.

00:30:37.707 --> 00:30:38.969
- Burrell Smith.

00:30:38.969 --> 00:30:41.024
- Oh, sorry for the mess.

00:30:41.024 --> 00:30:43.257
We're just a bunch of degenerates, so.

00:30:43.257 --> 00:30:44.090
- It's okay.

00:30:44.090 --> 00:30:45.243
I like degenerates.

00:30:45.243 --> 00:30:46.076
- Yeah.

00:30:46.979 --> 00:30:48.266
- Who's your project lead?

00:30:48.266 --> 00:30:49.683
- Uh, Jef Raskin.

00:30:51.163 --> 00:30:53.200
I don't know where he is.

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:56.008
- Jef Raskin. (exhales)

00:30:56.008 --> 00:30:58.091
Okay, I'll deal with him.

00:30:58.971 --> 00:31:01.510
I'll be taking over now.

00:31:01.510 --> 00:31:05.677
- Um, sorry, taking over, like
the entire Macintosh project?

00:31:06.911 --> 00:31:07.744
- Yeah.

00:31:07.744 --> 00:31:08.577
- Awesome.

00:31:10.509 --> 00:31:12.759
- Where's your design team?

00:31:15.434 --> 00:31:16.267
- Team?

00:31:17.998 --> 00:31:20.248
- Show me your credibility.

00:31:22.415 --> 00:31:24.737
- Well, obviously, ease of
use is a big feature here.

00:31:24.737 --> 00:31:26.998
We're trying to develop
a simpler interface

00:31:26.998 --> 00:31:29.724
that's geared towards
the real layman end user,

00:31:29.724 --> 00:31:32.489
but we're kind of struggling
with the interface,

00:31:32.489 --> 00:31:36.159
and to be frank, the results have been--

00:31:36.159 --> 00:31:36.992
- Shit.

00:31:36.992 --> 00:31:37.825
- Yeah.

00:31:37.825 --> 00:31:39.963
- [Steve] What's it got under the hood?

00:31:39.963 --> 00:31:41.505
- (mumbles) kick ass stuff.

00:31:41.505 --> 00:31:45.088
64K RAM, 256 bitmap
display, Motorola 6809.

00:31:46.130 --> 00:31:48.001
We're going to run the 68,000.

00:31:48.001 --> 00:31:50.448
- But dude, that's Lisa's processor.

00:31:50.448 --> 00:31:52.365
- That's correct, dude.

00:31:53.663 --> 00:31:55.414
I don't care where the budget is.

00:31:55.414 --> 00:31:57.414
Give me that processor.

00:31:57.414 --> 00:31:58.971
- Done.

00:31:58.971 --> 00:32:00.883
- Hey, Steve.

00:32:00.883 --> 00:32:02.300
How are you, man?

00:32:04.659 --> 00:32:05.492
(Steve laughs)

00:32:05.492 --> 00:32:06.325
- Chris Espinosa.

00:32:06.325 --> 00:32:07.158
- Yep.

00:32:07.158 --> 00:32:08.738
- You're working on the Macintosh?

00:32:08.738 --> 00:32:09.571
- Yeah.

00:32:10.571 --> 00:32:11.404
- Good.

00:32:12.346 --> 00:32:14.513
Then this is going to be fun.

00:32:20.966 --> 00:32:23.947
Okay, let's go back to what
you were saying before.

00:32:23.947 --> 00:32:27.211
This thing is for the everyman, right?

00:32:27.211 --> 00:32:28.878
That's our end user.

00:32:30.022 --> 00:32:31.914
It's the schoolteacher.

00:32:31.914 --> 00:32:33.450
It's the garbage man.

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:34.283
It's the kid.

00:32:34.283 --> 00:32:37.116
It's some grandma out in Nebraska.

00:32:37.994 --> 00:32:41.077
So we need to make this thing simple.

00:32:42.472 --> 00:32:44.222
It's got to work like,

00:32:46.683 --> 00:32:48.183
like an appliance.

00:32:50.698 --> 00:32:52.292
- Hey, Jef.

00:32:52.292 --> 00:32:54.408
Steve Jobs is here.

00:32:54.408 --> 00:32:56.658
Since you weren't around...

00:32:57.694 --> 00:32:59.450
- Some of the project leads

00:32:59.450 --> 00:33:02.722
were having a meeting with Motorola.

00:33:02.722 --> 00:33:04.932
Why exactly are you here?

00:33:04.932 --> 00:33:08.886
- I'm here to help build the Macintosh.

00:33:08.886 --> 00:33:11.952
- I'm not sure that
that's practical for us.

00:33:11.952 --> 00:33:14.795
We're actually trying
to avoid this project

00:33:14.795 --> 00:33:18.045
becoming another over bloated disaster.

00:33:23.007 --> 00:33:24.007
- Okay, Jef.

00:33:27.924 --> 00:33:29.674
You got my attention.

00:33:32.437 --> 00:33:35.771
- Look, Steve, you didn't
want to be involved

00:33:35.771 --> 00:33:39.821
back when we started this
thing, and that was fine by me.

00:33:39.821 --> 00:33:42.492
I just wanted you to cut the checks.

00:33:42.492 --> 00:33:44.930
Be involved, just don't turn Macintosh

00:33:44.930 --> 00:33:48.225
into a measure of revenge for you.

00:33:48.225 --> 00:33:51.442
Everyone knows about your
fallout with Team Lisa,

00:33:51.442 --> 00:33:54.775
so trust me when I say we're doing fine.

00:33:57.983 --> 00:33:58.983
- Okay, Jef,

00:34:02.439 --> 00:34:05.356
let's get a couple things straight.

00:34:06.578 --> 00:34:07.995
We don't do fine.

00:34:10.257 --> 00:34:12.929
And we don't accept things
the way that they are,

00:34:12.929 --> 00:34:15.346
and we don't stop innovating.

00:34:17.244 --> 00:34:19.678
Your machine lacks power,

00:34:19.678 --> 00:34:22.401
and your little interface, it needs work.

00:34:22.401 --> 00:34:24.515
And I'm here to help with that.

00:34:24.515 --> 00:34:28.248
Now I'm not trying to take
Macintosh away from you.

00:34:28.248 --> 00:34:30.248
I want you on this team,

00:34:31.498 --> 00:34:35.665
but you can either get on board
or you can get the fuck out.

00:34:43.721 --> 00:34:47.180
Okay, now we got a lot of work
to do, so let's get to it.

00:34:47.180 --> 00:34:48.738
Bill, you come with me.

00:34:48.738 --> 00:34:49.696
- Okay.

00:34:49.696 --> 00:34:50.719
Where are we going?

00:34:50.719 --> 00:34:52.479
- Recruiting.

00:34:52.479 --> 00:34:53.760
- Shit.

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:56.260
- Do you know why people buy an Apple?

00:34:56.260 --> 00:35:00.505
Why do they buy an Apple,
not the competitor?

00:35:00.505 --> 00:35:02.732
Because it's got bravado.

00:35:02.732 --> 00:35:04.469
It's social status.

00:35:04.469 --> 00:35:05.302
No. You know what?

00:35:05.302 --> 00:35:06.260
It's even more than that.

00:35:06.260 --> 00:35:08.173
It's social currency.

00:35:08.173 --> 00:35:10.250
We've raised the bar.

00:35:10.250 --> 00:35:14.333
If we want to stay there,
we got to risk everything.

00:35:16.733 --> 00:35:20.900
Great artists, Dylan, Picasso,
Newton, they risk failure,

00:35:22.417 --> 00:35:26.417
and if we want to be great,
we got to risk it, too.

00:35:28.017 --> 00:35:30.600
Everything is a pressing issue.

00:35:32.455 --> 00:35:36.372
If we want to make the
vision for Lisa a reality,

00:35:37.649 --> 00:35:40.750
we got to put in the hours
and make something great.

00:35:40.750 --> 00:35:43.894
- Yeah, but we have a hard
date on this software,

00:35:43.894 --> 00:35:45.939
and I'm sorry, but adding pretty fonts

00:35:45.939 --> 00:35:48.022
is not going to change that.

00:35:49.411 --> 00:35:50.244
Bill?

00:35:51.107 --> 00:35:54.774
- If you don't share
our enthusiasm and care

00:35:56.969 --> 00:35:58.963
for the vision of this company--

00:35:58.963 --> 00:35:59.796
- No, no, no, no, no.

00:35:59.796 --> 00:36:01.163
It's just, I'm not understanding--

00:36:01.163 --> 00:36:01.996
- Get out.

00:36:01.996 --> 00:36:02.829
- What?

00:36:03.704 --> 00:36:05.911
- Get your shit and get out.

00:36:05.911 --> 00:36:07.494
You're done.

00:36:07.494 --> 00:36:09.016
- What, are you going to fire me?

00:36:09.016 --> 00:36:10.245
- No!

00:36:10.245 --> 00:36:11.912
I already fired you!

00:36:13.465 --> 00:36:15.382
Why are you still here?

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.924
- Steve, he was our best
programmer in the division.

00:36:25.924 --> 00:36:27.538
- He's the best programmer

00:36:27.538 --> 00:36:30.673
that doesn't care about our vision.

00:36:30.673 --> 00:36:32.923
(laughter)

00:36:34.237 --> 00:36:35.194
(Red clears throat)

00:36:35.194 --> 00:36:37.444
(laughter)

00:36:38.551 --> 00:36:39.468
- Red, hey.

00:36:40.996 --> 00:36:42.433
(laughter)

00:36:42.433 --> 00:36:44.723
You're wondering why I'm
going through your stuff.

00:36:44.723 --> 00:36:46.763
(laughter)

00:36:46.763 --> 00:36:47.596
Okay.

00:36:48.437 --> 00:36:49.437
That's, see,

00:36:51.983 --> 00:36:54.233
I needed to borrow your saw

00:36:57.467 --> 00:37:00.300
because I need to chop down a tree

00:37:01.190 --> 00:37:04.166
(laughter)

00:37:04.166 --> 00:37:07.685
because there's something stuck in it,

00:37:07.685 --> 00:37:09.559
(laughter)

00:37:09.559 --> 00:37:11.177
an animal, a rabbit.

00:37:11.177 --> 00:37:13.427
(laughter)

00:37:15.922 --> 00:37:18.667
There's a rabbit stuck in a tree,

00:37:18.667 --> 00:37:19.696
(laughter)

00:37:19.696 --> 00:37:23.363
and I want to return
that rabbit to the wild

00:37:24.447 --> 00:37:26.041
so it can lay its eggs.

00:37:26.041 --> 00:37:28.291
(laughter)

00:37:35.480 --> 00:37:37.730
- Council, rabbits don't...

00:37:38.640 --> 00:37:40.471
How the hell did a rabbit get up a tree?

00:37:40.471 --> 00:37:42.261
(laughter)

00:37:42.261 --> 00:37:45.417
- [Michael] Uh, Eric threw it up there.

00:37:45.417 --> 00:37:47.667
(laughter)

00:37:54.012 --> 00:37:56.953
- Eric threw a rabbit up a tree?

00:37:56.953 --> 00:37:59.041
(laughter)

00:37:59.041 --> 00:37:59.874
- Yeah.

00:38:00.904 --> 00:38:02.773
He's a sadistic bastard.

00:38:02.773 --> 00:38:05.023
(laughter)

00:38:06.126 --> 00:38:07.760
You know he had a cow?

00:38:07.760 --> 00:38:10.010
(laughter)

00:38:12.509 --> 00:38:15.259
(peaceful music)

00:38:37.495 --> 00:38:39.986
- Who changes these light bulbs?

00:38:39.986 --> 00:38:41.965
It must be a nightmare.

00:38:41.965 --> 00:38:43.166
- Adam.

00:38:43.166 --> 00:38:43.999
- Oh, here.

00:38:43.999 --> 00:38:46.156
You want the coat?

00:38:46.156 --> 00:38:47.239
Are you cold?

00:38:51.431 --> 00:38:53.392
- I can't do this.

00:38:53.392 --> 00:38:54.997
Could you please take me back to work?

00:38:54.997 --> 00:38:56.506
- No.

00:38:56.506 --> 00:38:57.543
No, that's not the deal.

00:38:57.543 --> 00:39:00.435
The deal is that we're
going to walk around

00:39:00.435 --> 00:39:02.988
and we're going to look at
the twinkly lights and then--

00:39:02.988 --> 00:39:05.771
- Why are you messing everything up?

00:39:05.771 --> 00:39:08.279
- That's what people do.

00:39:08.279 --> 00:39:10.456
They buy each other flowers
and they go to museums--

00:39:10.456 --> 00:39:11.289
- You know me.

00:39:11.289 --> 00:39:13.774
This stuff freaks me out.

00:39:13.774 --> 00:39:14.607
It's fake.

00:39:16.746 --> 00:39:18.434
What's wrong with what we're doing?

00:39:18.434 --> 00:39:19.267
It's working.

00:39:19.267 --> 00:39:20.225
We don't have to fight.

00:39:20.225 --> 00:39:21.058
We don't have to--

00:39:21.058 --> 00:39:21.891
- Maybe I want to fight.

00:39:21.891 --> 00:39:22.724
- Yeah, well I don't.

00:39:22.724 --> 00:39:23.807
- What are you going to do?

00:39:23.807 --> 00:39:25.989
You're never going to feel anything?

00:39:25.989 --> 00:39:27.466
How are you going to do that?

00:39:27.466 --> 00:39:28.520
- I don't know.

00:39:28.520 --> 00:39:30.103
I'll figure it out.

00:39:33.962 --> 00:39:35.254
- You're so messed up.

00:39:35.254 --> 00:39:36.440
- Yeah?

00:39:36.440 --> 00:39:38.958
I don't need you to take care of me.

00:39:38.958 --> 00:39:41.459
I take care of myself.

00:39:41.459 --> 00:39:42.876
That's what I do.

00:39:43.884 --> 00:39:46.954
Why don't you go find some other girl

00:39:46.954 --> 00:39:49.037
who's not going to hurt you?

00:39:50.978 --> 00:39:52.728
- Because I love you.

00:39:53.723 --> 00:39:54.556
- It's obvious.

00:39:54.556 --> 00:39:55.692
I completely love you.

00:39:55.692 --> 00:39:56.900
There.

00:39:56.900 --> 00:39:58.232
You're such a wimp.

00:39:58.232 --> 00:39:59.065
- [Emma] I am not a wimp.

00:39:59.065 --> 00:40:00.740
- Well then be with me.

00:40:00.740 --> 00:40:02.499
- Okay, you're going to fight me.

00:40:02.499 --> 00:40:03.470
Is that the way you're going to fight?

00:40:03.470 --> 00:40:04.453
You can't fight me.

00:40:04.453 --> 00:40:05.286
You're miniature.

00:40:05.286 --> 00:40:07.953
You're like a girl Rick Moranis.

00:40:09.174 --> 00:40:10.967
You fight like a hamster.

00:40:10.967 --> 00:40:11.884
- Hey, hey.

00:40:13.620 --> 00:40:15.940
Excuse me, excuse me.

00:40:15.940 --> 00:40:18.226
You can't do that here.

00:40:18.226 --> 00:40:19.329
- [Emma] Okay.

00:40:19.329 --> 00:40:20.162
I'm sorry.

00:40:22.472 --> 00:40:25.436
(acoustic guitar music)

00:40:25.436 --> 00:40:28.372
(Emma grunts)

00:40:28.372 --> 00:40:30.372
- All right, you're out.

00:40:46.999 --> 00:40:49.166
- I can't keep doing this.

00:40:51.886 --> 00:40:53.979
♫ Put your hands in the water

00:40:53.979 --> 00:40:55.059
I'm not going to see you again.

00:40:55.059 --> 00:40:57.518
♫ Watch them go under

00:40:57.518 --> 00:40:58.351
- I know.

00:40:59.875 --> 00:41:01.670
That makes sense.

00:41:01.670 --> 00:41:05.051
♫ Put your hands to the light

00:41:05.051 --> 00:41:07.884
♫ Watch the light

00:41:08.886 --> 00:41:10.282
- Bye.

00:41:10.282 --> 00:41:12.865
♫ Come through

00:41:16.034 --> 00:41:19.367
♫ And I will let you go

00:41:25.759 --> 00:41:29.092
♫ And I will let you go

